ined with *user_path* if and only if the result is\n    contained within *root* both lexically and physically.\n\n    Raises ValueError for absolute user paths, any parent-directory\n    component (\"..\"), paths that resolve to the root itself, and joins\n    that escape root through symlinks (including when the final target\n    does not exist yet).\n    \"\"\"\n    root_path = Path(root)\n    user = Path(user_path)\n\n    if user.is_absolute():\n        raise ValueError(f\"absolute paths are not allowed: {user_path!r}\")\n\n    parts = user.parts\n    if not parts:\n        raise ValueError(\"path refers to the root itself: {user_path!r}\".format(user_path=user_path))\n    if \"..\" in parts:\n        raise ValueError(f\"parent traversal is not allowed: {user_path!r}\")\n\n    joined = root_path.joinpath(*parts)\n\n    real_root = root_path.resolve()\n    real_joined = joined.resolve()\n\n    if real_joined == real_root or real_root not in real_joined.parents:\n        raise ValueError(f\"path escapes root via symlinks: {user_path!r}\")\n\n    return joined\n```\n\nWait — `user.parts` on `Path('.')` → () → \"root itself\" — covers '', '.', './'. Good.\n\nHmm, one thing: `Path(user_path)` where user_path is already a Path with `..` handled? parts contains '..' → rejected. Good.\n\nBut wait, is there an issue with `parts` when user_path has a leading './'? `Path('./a').parts` → ('a',). Good.\n\nAlso `Path('a/../b').parts` → ('a', '..', 'b') → '..' in parts → raise. Good.\n\nNow `root_path.joinpath(*parts)`: parts are pure names (no separators, no '.', no '..'). Result = lexical join. For the public test: `Path('/tmp/root').joinpath('a','b') == Path('/tmp/root/a/b') == Path('/tmp/root')/'a/b'`. ✓.\n\nNow — subtle: `joined.resolve()` — if `root_path` is relative and cwd contains... resolve handles. ✓.\n\nAnother subtlety: what if root itself contains `..` or `.` components, like `Path('/tmp/root/../root2')`? root_path.resolve() resolves them. joined = root_path.joinp