ution is solid. Let me also consider adding `os.O_NOFOLLOW`-style physical verification... no, out of scope.\n\nOne more thought on the containment check strictness: task says returns path \"contained under root\" and \"Reject ... root itself\" — strict containment (child of root, not root). My check requires real_root ∈ parents(real_joined) — strict ancestor ✓.\n\nAlright, also think: should the lexical check happen against root AFTER normalizing root lexically (e.g., root='/tmp/root/../root')? With '..' banned in user_path only, and physical check via resolve, root's own '..' components get resolved in real_root. joined lexical = '/tmp/root/../root/a' — resolve → '/tmp/root/a' (if root2… wait '/tmp/root/../root' resolves to '/tmp/root'). Contained ✓. Return lexical joined. Fine.\n\nEdge: root='/tmp/root/../other' and user='a': real_root='/tmp/other', real_joined='/tmp/other/a' ✓.\n\nImplementation detail: compute `real_root` once — fine (single call).\n\nMicro-optimization: skip resolve if root doesn't exist? Not needed.\n\nNow, code:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Safely join a user-supplied relative path onto a trusted root directory.\"\"\"\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n__all__ = [\"safe_join\"]\n\n\ndef safe_join(root, user_path):\n    \"\"\"Join ``user_path`` onto ``root``, refusing anything unsafe.\n\n    Returns the joined ``Path`` only when the result is contained strictly\n    below ``root`` both lexically and physically (after resolving symlinks,\n    including for final components that do not exist yet).\n\n    Raises ValueError if ``user_path`` is absolute, contains a parent\n    directory component (``..``), refers to ``root`` itself, or escapes\n    ``root`` through a symlink.\n    \"\"\"\n    root_path = Path(root)\n    user = Path(user_path)\n\n    if user.is_absolute():\n        raise ValueError(f\"absolute paths are not allowed: {user_path!r}\")\n\n    parts = user.parts\n    if not parts:\n        raise ValueError(f\"path refers to the root itself: {user_path!r}\")\n    if \"..\" in parts:\n        raise ValueError(f\"parent traversal is not allowed: {user_path!r}\")\n\n    joined = root_path.joinpath(*parts)\n\n    real_root = root_path.resolve(strict=False)\n    real_joined = joined.resolve(strict=False)\n    if real_joined == real_root or real_root not in real_joined.parents:\n        raise ValueError(f\"path escapes root: {user_path!r}\")\n\n    return joined\n```\n\nHmm — `Path.resolve(strict=False)` keyword works on all 3.6+. ✓.\n\nWait, one thing about `resolve(strict=False)` raising RuntimeError on symlink loops in pathlib: pathlib._flavour.realpath... In 3.13 Path.resolve uses `os.path.realpath(self, strict=strict)`. os.path.realpath with strict=False on a loop: posixpath.realpath raises OSError(\"Circular symlink detected\")? Hmm — actually in 3.13, os.path.realpath's non-strict mode... Let me recall the implementation: `_joinrealpath` tracks `seen` dict; on cycle: \n\n```python\n            if path in seen:\n                path = seen[path]\n                ...\n```\nActually posixpath.realpath non-strict historically did NOT raise on loops — it returned the path with cycle un-resolved? There was a change: bpo-38453? Hmm. In CPython's posixpath._joinrealpath:\n\n```python\n        if islink(newpath):\n            path = newpath\n            if path in seen:\n                # Symlink loop\n                path = seen[path]  # or return current\n```\nI remember: non-strict realpath breaks cycles using the `seen` cache and returns something deterministic; strict mode raises OSError. And older pathlib resolve() (pre-3.6 era own implementation) raised RuntimeError on loops even non-strict? Whatever — worst case it raises an exception (rejection), best case it returns a resolved path that then fails containment. Either way safe. Moving on.\n\nAlso: `Path(user_path)` — if user_path is, say, an int → TypeError. Fine.\n\nDocstring: fine, concise.\n\nShould I keep the module docstring? Original file had none. Keep i